.TH RIG 1 "2026-09-19" "Knowledge Islands" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\&
.PP
rig \- describe and manage a personal working setup
.SH SYNOPSIS
\&
.br
.B rig show
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.br
.B rig list
.RI [ \-\-category " ID" ]
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.br
.B rig explain
.I TOOL
.br
.B rig status
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-unmanaged ]
.br
.B rig doctor
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.br
.B rig apply
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.br
.B rig bootstrap
.RI [ \-\-profile " NAME" ]
.RI [ \-\-dry-run ]
.br
.BI "rig run " PROVIDER " " ACTION
.RI [ \-\- " ARGUMENT..." ]
.br
.BI "rig export " PUBLICATION
.BI " \-\-output " DIRECTORY
.br
.BI "rig publish " PUBLICATION
.br
.B rig diag
.br
.BI "rig completion " bash|zsh
.br
.B rig help
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B rig
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\&
.PP
Rig is the declarative description and manager of a person's working setup.
Its catalogue records tools, categories, purposes, rationale, relationships,
supported platforms, profiles, and provider bindings.
.PP
Catalogue queries and diagnostics parse inert configuration and do not invoke
provider code. Status and doctor invoke observation capabilities.
Apply and bootstrap cross the explicit mutation boundary only after complete
plan preflight.
Run crosses an explicit provider-action trust boundary after validating its
declaration. Publish
crosses a network-capable trust boundary only after export validation.
Package and
configuration systems retain authority over their native manifests,
resolution, configuration, and state.
.SH INSTALLATION
\&
.PP
Install the
.B v0.2.0
public preview:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/knowledgeislands/tools-rig/v0.2.0/install.sh | bash
.fi
.PP
Pin an exact release explicitly:
.PP
.nf
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/knowledgeislands/tools-rig/v0.2.0/install.sh | bash -s -- v0.2.0
.fi
.PP
The installer writes the executable to
.I ~/.local/bin
and the manual beneath
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/man/man1
by default. Set
.B RIG_INSTALL_DIR
or
.B RIG_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
to choose different locations. Set
.B RIG_VERSION
to an exact
.B vX.Y.Z
release for automation compatibility. A positional version takes precedence.
Without either form, the installer discovers the latest GitHub release.
.SH COMMANDS
\&
.TP
.B rig show
Describe the resolved default profile with a bounded-width, aligned tool table.
.TP
.BI "rig show \-\-profile " NAME
Describe the named resolved profile with a bounded-width, aligned tool table
without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig list
List every declared catalogue tool in deterministic order.
.TP
.BI "rig list \-\-category " ID
Narrow the list to tools in the declared category.
.TP
.BI "rig list \-\-profile " NAME
Narrow the list to tools selected by the resolved profile.
The category and profile filters may be combined.
.TP
.BI "rig explain " TOOL
Show the tool's identity, category, purpose, rationale, supported platforms,
relationships, profile membership, and compatible provider binding.
.TP
.B rig status
Compare every tool in the resolved default profile with its selected built-in
or custom-provider observation. Catalogue-only tools are reported as neutral
unavailable rows.
.TP
.BI "rig status \-\-profile " NAME
Compare a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig status \-\-unmanaged
Additionally ask every provider declaring the
.B inventory
capability to enumerate its domain, and report each observed identity that no
binding declares. Unmanaged rows are informational and do not make the result
unhealthy; a provider that cannot inventory reports its own state and detail
instead. Reverse reconciliation answers the opposite question to the tool
table: not whether declared software is installed, but whether installed
software was ever declared.
.TP
.B rig doctor
Check the resolved default profile and print one compact operational health
answer. Doctor checks effective XDG path accessibility, provider availability,
and selected-tool observations. Catalogue-only and incompatible-platform tools
are informational.
.TP
.BI "rig doctor \-\-profile " NAME
Check a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
Doctor invokes only selected providers' exact declared
.B observe
capability; it never applies, repairs, publishes, or invokes unselected
providers.
.TP
.B rig apply
Preflight every selected bound tool, then invoke built-in or custom-provider
apply capabilities in dependency-first order. A failure skips only transitive
dependants; independent work continues.
.TP
.BI "rig apply \-\-profile " NAME
Apply a named resolved profile without changing the configured default.
.TP
.B rig apply \-\-dry-run
Perform complete preflight and print planned work without invoking a provider.
.TP
.B rig bootstrap
Materialise the configured bootstrap profile through the same resolver,
dependency order, complete preflight, dispatch, reporting, and failure
boundaries as
.BR "rig apply" .
An explicit
.BI "\-\-profile " NAME
takes precedence over
.BR bootstrap-profile ;
when that field is absent, bootstrap uses
.BR default-profile .
.B \-\-dry-run
prints the shared plan without invoking a provider.
.TP
.BI "rig run " PROVIDER " " ACTION " [\-\- " ARGUMENT... "]"
Invoke one configuration-defined action through its named custom provider.
An
.B observe
action uses the provider's observation verb; a
.B mutate
action uses its application verb. Configured arguments precede accepted
caller arguments. Rig passes provider output through and preserves its native
exit status.
.TP
.BI "rig export " PUBLICATION " \-\-output " DIRECTORY
Generate a deterministic, versioned
.I rig.json
artifact from the publication's explicitly selected, platform-neutral profile.
Export invokes no provider, publisher, or network command.
.TP
.BI "rig publish " PUBLICATION
Render and validate one isolated public-data export beneath the effective Rig
cache, then invoke the publication's selected trusted custom publisher exactly
once.
The publisher and receiving website own presentation, credentials, destination,
deployment, and rollback.
.TP
.B rig diag
Print a non-mutating diagnostic snapshot containing Rig and Bash versions,
the invoked executable, active platform, effective XDG directories, root
configuration and fragment count, configuration validity, schema, and default
profile when available. No provider is invoked.
.TP
.BI "rig completion " bash|zsh
Print completion source for the selected shell. With
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-help ,
print command usage without generating completion source.
.TP
.B rig help
Show top-level command help. The optional
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
forms produce the same output.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
\&
.PP
The
.B rig completion bash
and
.B rig completion zsh
print completion definitions for the shipped command and option surface.
.PP
For Bash, source the definition from the shell configuration:
.PP
.nf
source <(rig completion bash)
.fi
.PP
For Zsh, write the definition to a file named
.I _rig
in a directory on
.B fpath
before
.B compinit
runs. For example:
.PP
.nf
mkdir -p ~/.zsh/completions
rig completion zsh > ~/.zsh/completions/_rig
fpath=(~/.zsh/completions $fpath)
autoload -Uz compinit && compinit
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
\&
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR \-V , " \-\-version"
Print the Rig version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\&
.PP
Rig reads an optional
.I ${RIG_CONFIG_HOME}/rig.toml
first when present and then regular
.I ${RIG_CONFIG_HOME}/conf.d/*.toml
fragments in bytewise filename order. At least one source must exist, and the
merged declaration must contain exactly one
.B [rig]
table.
When
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, its default is
.IR ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig .
.PP
Configuration uses schema 1's bounded TOML grammar: named tables, the integer
schema value, single-line basic strings, single-line string arrays, blank lines,
and comments. Accepted files are valid TOML. Rig rejects unused TOML features
and does not source configuration, evaluate commands, interpret shell syntax,
or perform general environment expansion.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
\&
.TP
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
Override the Rig configuration directory.
.TP
.B RIG_DATA_HOME
Override the Rig data directory.
.TP
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
Override the Rig state directory.
.TP
.B RIG_CACHE_HOME
Override the Rig cache directory.
.TP
.B RIG_PLATFORM
Override the active catalogue platform used by profile and binding resolution.
By default Rig maps Bash
.B OSTYPE
values beginning with
.B darwin
to
.B macos
and values beginning with
.B linux
to
.BR linux .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Set the base configuration directory when
.B RIG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Set the base data directory when
.B RIG_DATA_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/share .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Set the base state directory when
.B RIG_STATE_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.local/state .
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Set the base cache directory when
.B RIG_CACHE_HOME
is unset. The fallback is
.IR $HOME/.cache .
.SH FILES
\&
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/rig.toml
Optional root configuration file. Schema 1 uses
.B [rig]
with
.B schema
and
.B default-profile
fields, plus optional
.BR bootstrap-profile ;
.B [category.ID]
with
.B name
and
.BR purpose ;
.B [tool.ID]
with
.BR name ,
.BR category ,
.BR purpose ,
.BR rationale ,
and a non-empty
.B platforms
string array, plus optional
.BR requires ,
.BR related ,
.BR alternatives ,
and
.B artifacts
string arrays; and
.B [profile.ID]
with
.B profiles
and
.B tools
string arrays. Provider and binding sections retain their native locator and
argument data without executing it while configuration is loaded.
.B [provider.ID]
requires
.B adapter
and accepts
.BR command ,
.BR executable ,
.BR manifest ,
and
.B arguments
and
.B capabilities
string arrays.
For a
.B custom
provider, omitted
.B executable
resolves exactly beneath the effective Rig data directory as
.IR providers/ID .
An explicit value overrides that convention. Rig does not search the provider
directory or execute adjacent files.
The execution engine supports built-in
.BR homebrew ,
.BR uv ,
.BR chezmoi ,
and
.B direct-download
adapters plus the executable
.B custom
adapter. Every action requires the exact declared
.B observe
or
.B apply
capability.
.B [tool.ID]
requires
.B install.provider
when materialised, together with
.B install.kind
and
.B install.locator
and accepts optional scalar
.B install.destination
and
.B install.checksum
fields plus
.B install.platforms
and
.B install.arguments
string arrays. A tool with no install provider is catalogue-only. Destination
and checksum are restricted to direct-download installations.
.B [publication.ID]
requires
.BR profile ,
.BR title ,
.BR base-url ,
and
.BR publisher .
Offline export does not invoke the publisher. Explicit publication requires
that provider to use the
.B custom
adapter and declare the exact
.B publish
capability.
.B [action.PROVIDER.NAME]
requires
.BR mode ,
and
.BR description .
It accepts
.BR platforms ,
.BR arguments ,
and
.BR allowed-arguments ,
and optional
.B argument-policy
fields. The mode is exactly
.B observe
or
.BR mutate .
Only the executable
.B custom
adapter dispatches actions in schema 1. Argument policy defaults to Rig's exact
allowlist; value
.B provider
delegates validation to that custom provider.
.PP
The
.B base-url
must be an absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL without a query or fragment. Rig appends
a trailing slash when omitted and projects it as
.B publication.canonical_url
metadata. Rig does not generate presentation or navigation.
.PP
A minimal catalogue is:
.PP
.nf
[rig]
schema = 1
default-profile = "default"
bootstrap-profile = "bootstrap"

[category.navigation]
name = "Navigation"
purpose = "Move through Knowledge Islands"

[tool.mgit]
name = "MGit"
category = "navigation"
purpose = "Navigate related repositories"
rationale = "Keeps repository context visible"
platforms = ["macos"]
install.provider = "homebrew"
install.kind = "formula"
install.locator = "mgit"
install.platforms = ["macos"]

[provider.homebrew]

adapter = "homebrew"
capabilities = ["observe", "apply"]

[profile.default]
tools = ["mgit"]

[profile.bootstrap]
tools = ["mgit"]
.fi
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/rig/conf.d/*.toml
Optional configuration fragments.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/rig
Default data directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/rig/providers/ID
Conventional executable for custom provider
.IR ID .
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/rig
Default state directory.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/rig
Default cache directory.
.SH PUBLIC DATA EXPORT
\&
.PP
The
.B rig export
writes exactly one regular, non-symlink
.I rig.json
file. Its top-level
.B format
is
.B rig-publication
and its integer
.B version
is 1. It contains publication metadata, profile identity, selected categories,
and selected tool identity, name, purpose, rationale, declared platforms, and
closed relationships. Category, tool, platform, and relationship values use
deterministic bytewise ordering.
.PP
The public profile is resolved independently of the active host platform while
each tool retains its declared platforms. Relationships are included only when
both endpoints are selected. Provider and binding configuration, private
profiles, observed state, credentials, and machine paths are not rendered.
Consumers should check both
.B format
and
.B version
before interpreting the remaining document.
.PP
Rig first renders a complete sibling staging tree and then replaces the named
output directory. It never merges with an existing tree. For safety, Rig
rejects output targets that are
.BR / ,
.BR . ,
.BR .. ,
symlinks, or non-directories. Review the generated tree before passing it to a
separate hosting or publisher system.
.SH PUBLICATION
\&
.B rig publish
performs the same validated public projection in an isolated directory beneath
.IR ${RIG_CACHE_HOME:-${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/rig}/publish ,
then crosses one explicit network-capable trust boundary. It invokes no fallback
publisher and passes the selected provider one absolute complete-tree path.
.PP
Successful publication removes the staged tree. A publisher failure preserves
its native exit status and retains the complete tree, reporting its path on
standard error. An interruption before the export is complete removes its
incomplete known files, invokes no publisher, and returns the conventional
signal status without reporting a retained export. Once complete, an
interrupted publication instead retains and reports the tree. Validation,
staging, and render failures invoke no publisher.
.PP
Cleanup revalidates the canonical cache parent and operates relative to that
pinned directory. It unlinks only
.I rig.json
before removing the empty directory. A substituted parent, symlink,
unexpected file, or other unsafe shape fails closed without recursive traversal.
.SH PROVIDER EXECUTION
\&
.PP
Built-in adapters map selected bindings to native commands. Homebrew
.B formula
and
.B cask
bindings use
.BR brew (1);
.B mas
bindings use
.BR mas (1).
The
.B uv
adapter supports
.B tool
bindings, and the
.B chezmoi
adapter supports
.B target
bindings. Their native observation and application commands are respectively:
.PP
.nf
brew list --formula|--cask --versions LOCATOR
brew install --formula|--cask LOCATOR
mas list
mas install LOCATOR
uv tool list
uv tool install LOCATOR
chezmoi status --path-style=absolute -- LOCATOR
chezmoi apply -- LOCATOR
.fi
.PP
Provider arguments precede the native command. Binding arguments precede the
locator. Each configured value remains one literal argument. An explicit
.B executable
replaces the adapter default.
.PP
A
.B direct-download
.B executable
binding requires an HTTPS locator, absolute destination, and lowercase
.B sha256:
checksum. Observation reads only the destination. Application uses
.BR curl (1)
to write a sibling temporary file, verifies SHA-256, sets executable mode, and
renames it over a regular destination. Rig refuses symlink and non-regular
destinations, restricts redirects to HTTPS, rechecks destination safety before
replacement, and removes failed temporary files.
.SS CUSTOM PROVIDER PROTOCOL
\&
.PP
The
.B custom
adapter requires one
.B executable
and an exact
.B capabilities
array. Capability values are atomic and are not split on commas.
.PP
Rig invokes a custom provider using this versioned literal-argument protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 VERB PROVIDER TOOL KIND LOCATOR [BINDING_ARGUMENT ...]
.fi
.PP
.I VERB
is exactly
.B observe
or
.BR apply .
Rig preserves every configured value as one argument and performs no
evaluation, shell expansion, or implicit word splitting.
.PP
For a declared provider action, Rig fixes
.I KIND
to
.B action
and
.I LOCATOR
to the declared action identity:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 VERB PROVIDER PROVIDER action ACTION
    [ACTION_ARGUMENT ...] [CALLER_ARGUMENT ...]
.fi
.PP
The action declaration authorises that exact provider/action pair. Each caller
argument must
exactly match one
.B allowed-arguments
array item before invocation unless
.B argument-policy
is
.BR provider .
In that case the provider validates its native domain. Provider stdout and
stderr pass through and the native exit status is preserved.
.PP
Publication uses the separate fixed protocol:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 publish PROVIDER PUBLICATION
    directory ABS_EXPORT_DIR
.fi
.PP
The publisher must declare the exact
.B publish
capability. Rig passes publisher output through and preserves its native result.
.PP
Reverse reconciliation uses a separate fixed protocol that names no tool,
because it enumerates a provider's whole domain rather than observing one
declaration:
.PP
.nf
EXECUTABLE [PROVIDER_ARGUMENT ...] rig-provider-v1 inventory PROVIDER
.fi
.PP
The provider must declare the exact
.B inventory
capability. Inventory stdout contains zero or more lines, each beginning with
an identity comparable to a binding
.BR locator ,
optionally followed by a tab and provider detail that Rig treats as opaque.
The separator is a tab because an identity may be a path containing spaces.
Rig reports every identity that no binding declares. A binding declares an
identity either through its
.B locator
, matched only within that binding's own provider namespace because a
Mac App
Store numeric identity and a cask name may coincide, or through a
repeatable tool
.B artifact
value naming a machine-observable path the tool materialises, matched
whichever provider observed it. A
non-zero exit yields state
.B unknown
with detail
.BR exit:N .
Inventory is an observation and must not mutate the machine.
.PP
Observation stdout must contain exactly one of
.BR present ,
.BR missing ,
.BR drifted ,
.BR unavailable ,
or
.BR unknown .
Provider stderr remains diagnostic output. Invalid observation output becomes
.B unknown
with detail
.BR invalid-response ;
a non-zero provider exit becomes
.B unknown
with detail
.BR exit:N .
Application output is routed to stderr so Rig alone owns deterministic stdout.
.SH DEVELOPMENT INSTALLATIONS
\&
.PP
For development against a local checkout, run
.B ./install.sh --link
to link the current executable and manual into
.B RIG_INSTALL_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/bin )
and
.B RIG_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
(default: the XDG data directory beneath
.IR man/man1 ).
The linked command follows changes to the checkout and does not download a
release. Re-run link mode after moving the checkout. Use
.B ./bin/rig
directly or
.B man -l man/rig.1
to inspect the checked-out artifacts without changing installed paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\&
.B rig run
returns the selected action provider's native status, including values
outside the Rig-owned statuses below.
.B rig publish
likewise returns the publisher's native non-zero status. Interrupted publication
returns 129, 130, or 143 for HUP, INT, or TERM. Rig-owned publication
validation, export, and cleanup failures return 2.
.TP
.B 0
Healthy status or doctor result, or successful apply or bootstrap, including
neutral catalogue-only rows.
.TP
.B 1
Valid unhealthy observation or doctor finding, provider runtime failure,
dependency suppression, or diagnostic finding. For
.B rig diag
this means configuration is missing or invalid.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command syntax, unknown query identity, invalid configuration,
resolution failure, or apply or bootstrap preflight failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
\&
.BR bash (1),
.BR brew (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR chezmoi (1),
.BR mas (1),
.BR shasum (1),
.BR uv (1)
